REFERENCE   1  (bases 1 to 14518)
  AUTHORS   Hann,S.R., King,M.W., Bentley,D.L., Anderson,C.W. and Eisenman,R.N.
  TITLE     A non-AUG translational initiation in c-myc exon 1 generates an
            N-terminally distinct protein whose synthesis is disrupted in
            Burkitt's lymphomas
  JOURNAL   Cell 52 (2), 185-195 (1988)
   PUBMED   3277717
COMMENT     REVIEWED REFSEQ: This record has been curated by NCBI staff in
            collaboration with Zahra Jalali, Liz Starks, Djamel Harbi. The
            reference sequence was derived from AC103819.3.
            This sequence is a reference standard in the RefSeqGene project.
            
            On Sep 20, 2017 this sequence version replaced NG_007161.1.
            
            Summary: This gene is a proto-oncogene and encodes a nuclear
            phosphoprotein that plays a role in cell cycle progression,
            apoptosis and cellular transformation. The encoded protein forms a
            heterodimer with the related transcription factor MAX. This complex
            binds to the E box DNA consensus sequence and regulates the
            transcription of specific target genes. Amplification of this gene
            is frequently observed in numerous human cancers. Translocations
            involving this gene are associated with Burkitt lymphoma and
            multiple myeloma in human patients. There is evidence to show that
            translation initiates both from an upstream, in-frame non-AUG (CUG)
            and a downstream AUG start site, resulting in the production of two
            isoforms with distinct N-termini. [provided by RefSeq, Aug 2017].
